WEBVTT

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:04.533
A play runs only after you've simulated
it and a person has signed it.

00:00:04.533 --> 00:00:07.400
Now the whole picture:
a buying signal arrives,

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:11.133
and a prospect gets the right
message from the right person,

00:00:11.133 --> 00:00:12.800
with every step approved.

00:00:12.833 --> 00:00:19.933
Over the next few minutes you'll
hold the whole loop in your head.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.533
Most teams run these as separate tools.

00:00:22.533 --> 00:00:24.967
Mia's best account replies on Tuesday.

00:00:24.967 --> 00:00:29.167
The reply sits in the sequencer,
the score never hears about it,

00:00:29.167 --> 00:00:32.467
and Leo exports a
spreadsheet to find out why.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:37.033
Every engine works on the same records,
reads the same signed definitions,

00:00:37.033 --> 00:00:40.333
and passes work through
the same approvals.

00:00:40.333 --> 00:00:45.267
That's what makes this a loop, not
a pile of tools joined by exports.

00:00:45.333 --> 00:00:49.833
First, a signal: a funding
round, a hire, a visit, a reply.

00:00:49.833 --> 00:00:53.400
The signals engine keeps the evidence
and checks it before it counts.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:54.567
In Strict mode,

00:00:54.567 --> 00:00:58.767
an unverified signal never scores
and never reaches a seller.

00:00:58.833 --> 00:00:59.900
Second, score.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:02.767
The engine weighs the signal against fit

00:01:02.767 --> 00:01:05.667
and returns a verdict with its evidence.

00:01:05.667 --> 00:01:09.567
Your definition of a good
account does its work here.

00:01:09.633 --> 00:01:17.067
Enrichment fills only the gaps that
matter, inside the limits you set.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:23.067
Routing picks the owner from territories
and capacity, and records why.

00:01:23.067 --> 00:01:27.567
Research adds a brief, so
that person starts informed.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:31.167
Third, draft. Outreach writes
from your proof and your voice,

00:01:31.167 --> 00:01:34.733
and every factual claim is checked
against evidence you approved.

00:01:34.733 --> 00:01:37.267
The check runs twice: when
the message is proposed,

00:01:37.267 --> 00:01:41.900
and again when it's sent. A stale
claim can't slip out between the two.

00:01:41.967 --> 00:01:47.267
Then the work stops at a gate. A
person who isn't the proposer approves,

00:01:47.267 --> 00:01:49.833
and the agent that prepared it can't.

00:01:49.833 --> 00:01:53.500
With Dry Mode on, the
message stays a draft.

00:01:53.533 --> 00:01:57.800
Once approved, it goes out inside
your guardrails: daily caps,

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:01.933
send windows in the recipient's
timezone, and the channels you allow.

00:02:01.933 --> 00:02:06.133
One message goes out, and it
makes sense for that prospect.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:11.600
The prospect replies. The reply is
labeled, and its effects run once.

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:16.200
An unsubscribe is respected,
a referral opens a task,

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.433
and a not now comes back later.

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:21.233
Last, the outcome. A
meeting booked, a deal won,

00:02:21.233 --> 00:02:24.267
or a wrong person reply
returns to the score engine.

00:02:24.267 --> 00:02:27.433
It compares what it
predicted with what happened,

00:02:27.433 --> 00:02:30.933
so you tune your definition
from evidence, not guesses.

00:02:30.933 --> 00:02:32.100
Pro move: before you trust an engine,

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.933
ask your agent for its shadow accuracy.

00:02:34.967 --> 00:02:39.200
Plays are the glue. A play
connects these steps on a trigger,

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:41.733
with the same guardrails and approvals,

00:02:41.733 --> 00:02:44.900
so the loop runs without hand wiring.

00:02:44.933 --> 00:02:49.233
Every step leaves a record you can
open: why an account scored as it did,

00:02:49.233 --> 00:02:53.267
why it went to that owner, who
approved the send, and what came back.

00:02:53.267 --> 00:02:54.500
In the console, open any of them.

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:57.967
Or ask your agent: "Walk me through
what happened to this account,

00:02:57.967 --> 00:03:00.333
from the first signal to the last reply".

00:03:00.367 --> 00:03:01.767
Someone on your team will say:

00:03:01.767 --> 00:03:04.367
a loop that learns sounds
like a loop that drifts.

00:03:04.367 --> 00:03:06.533
It can't rewrite your rules.

00:03:06.533 --> 00:03:10.467
The score engine shows the gap
between prediction and result,

00:03:10.467 --> 00:03:12.633
and a person edits and
signs the definition.

00:03:12.633 --> 00:03:14.367
Nothing changes until someone signs.

00:03:14.433 --> 00:03:17.600
Be honest about the edges:
account based programs,

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:20.700
lifecycle and reporting
engines aren't built yet.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:26.033
This loop is what works today, and
those can join the same line later.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:30.267
Say it back: One set of
records and signed definitions.

00:03:30.267 --> 00:03:34.933
A gate before anything leaves.
Outcomes that return to the score.

00:03:34.967 --> 00:03:38.667
You can now read any account's
story from start to finish.

00:03:38.667 --> 00:03:43.500
Ask your agent for one today. Next
up, Approvals and the human queue,

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:46.567
exactly what the person at the gate sees.
